{"pdfpcFormat":2,"disableMarkdown":false,"duration":30,"lastMinutes":2,"noteFontSize":12,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":1,"label":"1","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Welcome everyone. State your name and the topic. \n+ This presentation covers coalition logic and its quantified extension. \n+ The talk is structured in 8 parts, lasting about 30 minutes. \n+ You will use game-theoretic frameworks to reason about multi-agent systems. \n+ Timing: 0:00 to 0:30."},{"idx":2,"label":"2","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Briefly walk through the 8 sections. \n+ Emphasize the logical progression: single-agent modal logic, multi-agent, coalition logic, quantified coalition logic. \n+ Mention that Section 7 (complexity) and Section 8 (voting applications) tie everything together. \n+ Timing: 0:30 to 1:00."},{"idx":3,"label":"3","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":4,"label":"4","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Start with the familiar: Kripke models for single-agent modal logic. \n+ An agent at state s can move to state t if s R t. \n+ The modality ◇ φ expresses possibility: there exists an accessible state where φ holds. \n+ This is the foundation that we will generalize. \n+ Timing: 1:00 to 2:00."},{"idx":5,"label":"5","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":6,"label":"5","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false},{"idx":7,"label":"6","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the key motivation: standard multi-agent logics treat agents independently. \n+ But in reality, actions interact. If agent 1 moves left and agent 2 moves right, the result is a joint outcome. \n+ Game frames solve this by associating each state with a strategic game. \n+ The outcome function maps strategy profiles to states. \n+ This is the foundation of Coalition Logic. \n+ Timing: 2:00 to 4:00."},{"idx":8,"label":"7","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":9,"label":"8","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Define the formal building block: a strategic game. \n+ N is the set of players,  the strategies, S the states. \n+ The outcome function o takes a strategy profile (one strategy per player) and returns a state. \n+ This is a game form with no preferences yet, just the mechanics. \n+ Timing: 4:00 to 5:00."},{"idx":10,"label":"9","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ A game frame is a transition system where transitions are strategic games. \n+ At each state s, the function γ gives the game that agents must play. \n+ The outcome of that game determines the next state. \n+ This is more general than Kripke frames: instead of a simple relation, we have a full game. \n+ Timing: 5:00 to 6:00."},{"idx":11,"label":"10","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the central concept: effectivity functions. \n+ X ∈  means coalition C can force the outcome into set X. \n+ The quantifier structure is key: EXISTS a strategy for C, FOR ALL strategies of the opponents. \n+ This captures the idea of \"forcing\": the coalition has a winning strategy. \n+ The logic will reason about these effectivity functions directly. \n+ Timing: 6:00 to 8:00."},{"idx":12,"label":"11","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ These three properties ensure the effectivity function comes from a real game. \n+ Outcome-monotonicity: if you can guarantee winning, you can guarantee \"someone wins.\" \n+ Superadditivity: disjoint coalitions can combine their strategies, cooperation pays off. \n+ N-maximality: if nobody can prevent X, then everyone together can achieve it. \n+ These properties will be reflected as axioms in the logic. \n+ Timing: 8:00 to 9:00."},{"idx":13,"label":"12","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":14,"label":"13","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Introduce the modal operator of Coalition Logic. \n+ The double angle brackets denote the coalition ability operator. \n+ It is read as \"C can achieve phi.\" \n+ We use this notation to avoid confusion with the universal quantifier [P]φ in QCL. \n+ Timing: 9:00 to 10:00."},{"idx":15,"label":"14","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":16,"label":"14","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Frames give structure; models add truth values for propositions. \n+ The semantics is elegant: C can force φ iff the set of φ-states is in the effectivity of C at s. \n+ This directly connects the logic to the game-theoretic notion of effectivity. \n+ The formula  is the set of states where φ is true. \n+ Timing: 10:00 to 11:00."},{"idx":17,"label":"15","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the most important theoretical result in CL. \n+ It bridges two worlds: the abstract algebraic world of effectivity functions, and the concrete world of strategic games. \n+ The forward direction is straightforward: every game induces a playable effectivity function. \n+ The reverse direction is the deep one: every playable function comes from some game. \n+ This justifies working directly with effectivity functions in the logic. \n+ Timing: 11:00 to 12:00."},{"idx":18,"label":"16","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":19,"label":"16","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"+ The forward direction is almost immediate from the definitions. \n+ For superadditivity: if  and  are disjoint, their strategies do not conflict, so they can be played simultaneously. \n+ For the reverse direction: the construction is non-trivial. You build a game where each coalition's \"power\" matches what E declares. \n+ The three playability properties are essential for the construction to be consistent. \n+ Timing: 12:00 to 14:00."},{"idx":20,"label":"17","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":21,"label":"18","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ These axioms mirror the playability properties. \n+ Consistency: nothing can force a contradiction. \n+ Efficacy: every coalition can always do something. \n+ g-maximality: what the empty coalition cannot make inevitable, the grand coalition can achieve. \n+ Superadditivity is the most critical: it captures the ability to combine strategies. \n+ This axiom is what makes satisfiability PSPACE-complete rather than NP-complete. \n+ Timing: 14:00 to 15:00."},{"idx":22,"label":"19","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the key limitation that motivates QCL. \n+ In CL, you must enumerate every possible coalition explicitly. \n+ For n agents, there are  coalitions, exponential blowup. \n+ A simple property like \"some majority can force φ\" requires listing all majority coalitions. \n+ For n = 100 voters, this is astronomically large. \n+ Timing: 15:00 to 17:00."},{"idx":23,"label":"20","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":24,"label":"21","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":25,"label":"21","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false},{"idx":26,"label":"22","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ QCL's innovation is not more expressive power, but more concise representation. \n+ Predicates describe properties of coalitions: is this coalition a subset of D? Does it contain D? \n+ The cardinality predicate is crucial: it identifies all coalitions of size at least n. \n+ Majority becomes a single predicate instead of an exponential disjunction. \n+ Timing: 17:00 to 19:00."},{"idx":27,"label":"23","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ The existential ⟨ P ⟩ φ: SOME coalition with property P can force φ. \n+ The universal [P] φ: ALL coalitions with property P can force φ. \n+ Important: [P] is NOT simply the dual of ⟨ P ⟩. The dual would be ∀ ∃, but [P] follows ∀ ∃ ∀, the correct quantifier pattern for collective ability. \n+ This subtlety is essential for modeling coalition power correctly. \n+ Timing: 19:00 to 21:00."},{"idx":28,"label":"23","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"+ The existential ⟨ P ⟩ φ: SOME coalition with property P can force φ. \n+ The universal [P] φ: ALL coalitions with property P can force φ. \n+ Important: [P] is NOT simply the dual of ⟨ P ⟩. The dual would be ∀ ∃, but [P] follows ∀ ∃ ∀, the correct quantifier pattern for collective ability. \n+ This subtlety is essential for modeling coalition power correctly. \n+ Timing: 19:00 to 21:00."},{"idx":29,"label":"24","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":30,"label":"24","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the main theoretical result of the paper by Agotnes, van der Hoek, and Wooldridge. \n+ QCL and CL have the same expressive power: nothing new is expressible. \n+ But QCL is exponentially more succinct: the same property can be stated with much shorter formulas. \n+ The proof compares the length of formulas in both logics for the majority property. \n+ In CL, you must list all majority coalitions. In QCL, just one predicate. \n+ Timing: 21:00 to 23:00."},{"idx":31,"label":"25","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":32,"label":"26","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":33,"label":"26","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false},{"idx":34,"label":"27","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ The superadditivity axiom is what makes CL harder than propositional logic. \n+ You cannot just check each coalition independently; you must consider how they combine. \n+ The surprise: despite being exponentially more succinct, QCL has the SAME complexity as CL. \n+ This means QCL is strictly better for humans (shorter formulas) without being worse for machines. \n+ This is a rare and desirable property in logic. \n+ Timing: 23:00 to 26:00."},{"idx":35,"label":"28","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":36,"label":"29","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":37,"label":"29","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"+ This is the crown jewel application: voting systems. \n+ In QCL, you can specify a complete majority voting system in 4 short formulas. \n+ The predicates capture the essence: \"any group of size > n/2 can determine the outcome.\" \n+ Compare this to CL, where each formula would need to list all majority coalitions explicitly. \n+ For n = 100, the CL version would be astronomically long. \n+ Timing: 26:00 to 28:00."},{"idx":38,"label":"30","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Summary table comparing CL and QCL. \n+ Same expressive power, same complexity, but QCL is exponentially more succinct. \n+ This makes QCL the practical choice for specifying multi-agent systems. \n+ The theoretical foundation (effectivity functions, game frames) remains the same. \n+ Timing: 28:00 to 29:00."},{"idx":39,"label":"31","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":40,"label":"32","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":41,"label":"32","overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false},{"idx":42,"label":"33","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Conclude by emphasizing the unifying power of CL. \n+ Traditional modal logic is just the 1-player case of Coalition Logic. \n+ QCL bridges theory and practice: you can now specify and verify real systems. \n+ \"Social Software\" is Pauly's vision: using logic to design and verify social institutions. \n+ Thank the audience. Open for questions (10 minutes). \n+ Timing: 29:00 to 30:00."},{"idx":43,"label":"33","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"+ Thank the audience for their attention. \n+ Remind them of the 10-minute Q&A session. \n+ Be prepared to explain: \n  - Why superadditivity makes CL PSPACE-complete. \n  - The difference between [P]φ and ¬ ⟨ P ⟩ ¬ φ. \n  - How to read speaker notes with pdfpc."}]}
